BEGIN;

INSERT INTO questions (section_id, topic, stem, choices, correct_index, explanation, difficulty, cognitive_level)
VALUES

-- Q1: hard / L4 — Contradictory evidence vs management representations
(1, 'Written Representations', 'During the audit of Northbrook Industries'' December 31, 2025 financial statements, the engagement partner at Sullivan & Clark CPA obtains a signed management representation letter stating that all transactions have been recorded. However, the auditor discovers unrecorded vendor invoices totaling $380,000, which exceeds performance materiality. The auditor should evaluate whether to:', '["Request management to revise the representation letter to exclude the completeness assertion", "Treat the unrecorded invoices as an isolated clerical error and accept the representations", "Consider the implications for the reliability of all management representations and assess the effect on the auditor''s report", "Accept the representation as sufficient evidence that the remaining transactions are complete"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under AU-C 580.A20, when audit evidence contradicts a written representation, the auditor must investigate the circumstances, reconsider the reliability of other representations, and determine the effect on the audit opinion. The contradictory evidence calls into question whether management''s representations can be relied upon more broadly. Simply accepting the representation (choice D) ignores the auditor''s obligation to exercise professional skepticism when contradictions arise.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q2: easy / L2 — Representation letter addressed to the auditor
(1, 'Written Representations', 'Fletcher & Associates CPA receives the management representation letter from Willow Creek Corp. for the 2025 audit. Under AU-C 580, the letter should be addressed to:', '["The entity''s board of directors", "The auditor (Fletcher & Associates CPA)", "The SEC or applicable regulatory body", "The entity''s audit committee chair"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under AU-C 580.15, the management representation letter is addressed to the auditor because it serves as a direct communication from management to the audit engagement team. The letter is not addressed to the board (choice A) because representations flow from management to the auditor, not to governance bodies, even though governance may have oversight responsibilities.', 'easy', 2),

-- Q3: easy / L1 — Written representations are necessary but not sufficient evidence
(1, 'Written Representations', 'Under AU-C 580, written representations from management are considered what type of audit evidence?', '["Necessary audit evidence that does not substitute for other audit procedures", "The most reliable form of audit evidence available", "Optional supporting documentation for the audit file", "Sufficient evidence to support all financial statement assertions"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under AU-C 580.04, written representations are necessary audit evidence but do not provide sufficient appropriate evidence on their own about any of the matters with which they deal. The auditor must still perform other procedures. Considering them the most reliable form (choice B) is incorrect because external confirmations and direct observation rank higher in the evidence reliability hierarchy.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q4: medium / L3 — Change in management during audit period
(1, 'Written Representations', 'Midway through the December 2025 fiscal year, Riverton Dynamics LLC replaced its CEO and CFO. The new leadership team took office on July 1, 2025. When the engagement team at Drake & Associates requests the management representation letter, the new CEO questions whether she can represent the full fiscal year. What is the most likely effect on the representation letter?', '["The representation letter can only cover July 1 through December 31, 2025", "The auditor must obtain a separate representation from the former officers for January through June", "The auditor should withdraw from the engagement due to a scope limitation", "Current management must provide representations covering the entire period of the financial statements, relying on appropriate inquiries and review"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under AU-C 580.A11, when management changes during the period, current management provides representations covering the full financial statement period because the statements are their responsibility. They may rely on inquiries, reviews of predecessor records, and other information to support their representations. Limiting coverage to only their tenure (choice A) is incorrect because the financial statements cover the full year regardless of when management took office.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q5: medium / L2 — Representation about uncorrected misstatements
(1, 'Written Representations', 'During the audit of Pineview Holdings for the year ended December 31, 2025, the engagement team at Langford & Myers CPA identifies $18,500 in uncorrected misstatements, all below the $25,000 performance materiality threshold. Under AU-C 580, the management representation letter should include a representation that management:', '["Agrees to correct all misstatements before issuing the financial statements", "Believes the effects of uncorrected misstatements are immaterial, individually and in the aggregate, to the financial statements as a whole", "Authorizes the auditor to adjust the financial statements for the identified misstatements", "Acknowledges the misstatements but defers correction to the subsequent period"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under AU-C 580.11, the representation letter must include management''s acknowledgment that the effects of uncorrected misstatements aggregated during the audit are immaterial to the financial statements as a whole. A schedule of such misstatements is typically attached to the letter. Agreeing to correct all misstatements (choice A) describes a different management response that is not the required representation under AU-C 580.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q6: easy / L1 — Management responsibility for internal control
(1, 'Written Representations', 'Under AU-C 580, which representation must management provide regarding internal control?', '["That internal control eliminates all risk of material misstatement", "That no control deficiencies exist within the entity", "That management acknowledges its responsibility for the design, implementation, and maintenance of internal control", "That internal control has been evaluated by an independent third party"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under AU-C 580.10, management must represent that it acknowledges its responsibility for the design, implementation, and maintenance of internal control relevant to the preparation and fair presentation of financial statements. This does not mean controls are perfect. Claiming internal control eliminates all risk (choice A) is incorrect because internal control provides reasonable, not absolute, assurance.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q7: medium / L2 — Representation about compliance with laws and regulations
(1, 'Written Representations', 'Townsend Medical Group is completing its December 2025 audit with Brighton & Park CPA. The engagement partner asks management to provide a written representation about regulatory compliance. Under AU-C 580, management should represent that:', '["The entity has disclosed all known instances of noncompliance or suspected noncompliance with laws and regulations whose effects should be considered in preparing the financial statements", "The entity has never violated any laws or regulations during its existence", "A third-party legal review has confirmed full regulatory compliance", "The entity''s legal counsel has provided a written guarantee of compliance"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under AU-C 580.11, management is required to represent that it has disclosed to the auditor all known instances of noncompliance or suspected noncompliance with laws and regulations whose effects should be considered when preparing the financial statements. Representing that no violations have ever occurred (choice B) is incorrect because the standard requires disclosure of known noncompliance, not a blanket assertion of perfect compliance.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q8: easy / L1 — Inconsistency between representations and other evidence
(1, 'Written Representations', 'Under AU-C 580, when written representations are inconsistent with other audit evidence, the auditor should:', '["Accept the written representations as authoritative over other evidence", "Disregard the written representations entirely", "Inform the audit committee but take no further action", "Perform additional audit procedures to attempt to resolve the inconsistency"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under AU-C 580.A22, when written representations are inconsistent with other audit evidence, the auditor must perform additional procedures to resolve the matter. The auditor cannot simply accept management''s version (choice A) because professional skepticism requires investigation of conflicting evidence rather than defaulting to any single source.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q9: medium / L3 — Omission of fraud representation impacts engagement
(1, 'Written Representations', 'During the audit of Cascade Valley Enterprises for the year ended December 31, 2025, management provides a signed representation letter that omits the required representation about fraud. The engagement partner at Overton & Hayes CPA must analyze the effect of this omission. What is the most likely implication?', '["The auditor should issue an unmodified opinion with an emphasis-of-matter paragraph", "The auditor should treat the omission as a scope limitation and consider the effect on the auditor''s report, potentially issuing a disclaimer of opinion", "The auditor should perform additional fraud testing to compensate for the missing representation", "The auditor should accept the letter as provided since fraud representations are recommended but not required"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under AU-C 580.19-.20, if management does not provide one or more of the requested written representations, the auditor must discuss the matter with management and re-evaluate management''s integrity. If management still refuses, the auditor treats it as a scope limitation, which may result in a qualified opinion or disclaimer of opinion. Performing additional fraud testing (choice C) does not substitute for the required representation, which is mandatory under AU-C 580.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q10: medium / L2 — Representation about access to records and personnel
(1, 'Written Representations', 'Sterling Automotive Group provides its December 2025 management representation letter to the audit team at Crawford & Finch CPA. Under AU-C 580, which representation about access must the letter include?', '["That management retained an external consultant to organize all audit-relevant records", "That the auditor was permitted to observe all internal meetings during fieldwork", "That management has provided the auditor with access to all information relevant to the preparation and fair presentation of the financial statements", "That all records were stored in a single electronic database accessible to the auditor"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under AU-C 580.10, management must represent that it has provided the auditor with access to all information of which management is aware that is relevant to the preparation and fair presentation of the financial statements. This includes access to records, documents, and personnel. Retaining an external consultant (choice A) is not a requirement under the standard and does not satisfy the access representation.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q11: medium / L3 — Additional representations beyond standard requirements
(1, 'Written Representations', 'While auditing Greystone Properties LLC for the year ended December 31, 2025, the engagement partner at Whitmore & Associates identifies unusual equity transactions between Greystone and a related entity. The partner considers requesting an additional written representation beyond the standard letter. Under AU-C 580, which factor most likely supports the decision to request an additional representation?', '["The additional representation addresses a matter specific to the entity that the auditor considers necessary to support other audit evidence", "The auditor wants to reduce the number of substantive tests required", "The firm''s quality management policy mandates additional representations for all engagements", "The standard representation letter is too short to meet professional documentation requirements"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under AU-C 580.13, the auditor may request additional written representations about matters specific to the entity''s business or industry when deemed necessary to support other audit evidence. The request should address specific assertions relevant to the engagement. Reducing substantive tests (choice B) is incorrect because additional representations do not substitute for audit procedures and cannot be used to reduce testing.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q12: hard / L4 — Going concern representations vs contradictory evidence
(1, 'Written Representations', 'Mapleton Community Healthcare''s management provides a December 2025 representation letter asserting that its going concern plans — including a projected $4.2 million capital infusion from new investors — make liquidation unlikely. However, the auditor at Ashford & Kline CPA determines that the investor commitment is non-binding and the entity has negative working capital of $1.8 million. The auditor should:', '["Accept the representation and issue an unmodified opinion because management has provided a written plan", "Withdraw from the engagement since management''s representations are misleading", "Issue an adverse opinion because the financial statements should be prepared on a liquidation basis", "Evaluate whether substantial doubt about going concern exists, consider the adequacy of disclosure, and determine the appropriate opinion modification regardless of the representation"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under AU-C 580.A20 and AU-C 570, written representations about going concern do not override the auditor''s independent evaluation. The auditor must assess whether substantial doubt exists based on all available evidence, evaluate management''s plans (including their feasibility), and determine whether the financial statements require a going concern emphasis or opinion modification. Accepting the representation at face value (choice A) ignores the auditor''s duty to exercise professional skepticism when the supporting evidence is a non-binding commitment.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q13: medium / L3 — Intent and ability representations for held-to-maturity
(1, 'Written Representations', 'Brookfield Capital Partners holds $6.5 million in debt securities classified as held-to-maturity on its December 31, 2025 balance sheet. The engagement team at Mercer & Webb CPA must analyze whether the management representation letter adequately addresses this classification. Which representation is most critical to support the held-to-maturity classification?', '["That the securities were purchased during the current fiscal year", "That an independent valuation of the securities has been obtained", "That management has the positive intent and ability to hold the securities to maturity", "That the securities are traded on a recognized exchange"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under AU-C 580.A13, when financial statement items depend on management''s intent or ability, the auditor may request specific representations about management''s plans. For held-to-maturity classifications, management must represent both intent and financial ability to hold the securities to maturity, per ASC 320. Having an independent valuation (choice B) addresses fair value, not the intent-and-ability requirement that drives the held-to-maturity classification.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q14: medium / L3 — Contradictory evidence reduces reliability of representations
(1, 'Written Representations', 'Evergreen Distributing Co.''s management representation letter for the December 2025 audit states that all inventory is valued at the lower of cost or net realizable value. However, the audit team at Caldwell & Price CPA identified $215,000 in inventory items with NRV below recorded cost that management has not written down. How does this contradictory evidence most likely affect the auditor''s assessment?', '["The auditor should accept the representation because management may have information the auditor does not", "It calls into question the reliability of management''s other written representations and requires the auditor to reconsider the integrity of management", "The auditor should discard all written representations and rely solely on substantive testing", "The contradictory evidence has no effect since the representation letter constitutes a binding legal document"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under AU-C 580.A22, when audit evidence contradicts a written representation, the auditor must reconsider whether the representations are reliable and reassess management''s integrity. A contradiction about one assertion may signal broader concerns about the reliability of other representations. Accepting the representation without further inquiry (choice A) violates professional skepticism requirements because contradictory evidence demands investigation, not deference.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q15: easy / L1 — Representations about accounting estimates
(1, 'Written Representations', 'Under AU-C 580, the management representation letter should address accounting estimates by confirming that:', '["The methods, data, and significant assumptions used are reasonable in the context of the applicable financial reporting framework", "All estimates have been independently verified by a third-party actuary", "Estimates will not change in future reporting periods", "The entity has elected not to use estimates in its financial statements"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under AU-C 580.11 and AU-C 540, management must represent that the significant assumptions used in making accounting estimates are reasonable. The representation covers methods, underlying data, and key assumptions. Requiring independent third-party verification (choice B) is incorrect because many estimates are developed internally and do not require external validation to satisfy the representation requirement.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q16: medium / L2 — Initial audit and opening balance representations
(1, 'Written Representations', 'Canton & Wells CPA is conducting its first audit of Redfield Manufacturing for the year ended December 31, 2025. The predecessor auditor issued an unmodified opinion on the 2024 financial statements. Under AU-C 580, the management representation letter for this initial audit should:', '["Cover only the current-year financial statements since a predecessor auditor already opined on the prior year", "Be limited to matters discovered during fieldwork for the current period", "Exclude opening balance representations because they are the predecessor auditor''s responsibility", "Include representations about matters covering the full period of the financial statements, including opening balances relevant to the current period"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under AU-C 580.14, in an initial audit the auditor requests representations covering the periods of all financial statements presented, including opening balances. The predecessor auditor''s opinion does not relieve current management from providing representations. Covering only the current year (choice A) is incorrect because the auditor must obtain sufficient evidence about opening balances, which requires management''s representations about the full reporting period.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q17: medium / L2 — Representation about side agreements
(1, 'Written Representations', 'During the audit of Lakewood Financial Services for the year ended December 31, 2025, the engagement team at Garrett & Palmer CPA learns that Lakewood frequently enters into informal arrangements with counterparties. Under AU-C 580, the management representation letter should include a representation that:', '["All revenue has been independently verified by customers", "There are no side agreements, oral or written, that modify the terms of any recorded transactions or arrangements", "The entity has notified its legal counsel of all verbal commitments", "No oral communications with counterparties occurred during the year"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under AU-C 580.11, the representation letter should include management''s confirmation that there are no side agreements or other arrangements — oral or written — that have not been disclosed to the auditor and that could affect the financial statements. Claiming no oral communications occurred (choice D) is overbroad and unrealistic; the standard focuses on undisclosed side agreements that modify transaction terms, not on all verbal communications.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q18: medium / L2 — Representation about liens and encumbrances
(1, 'Written Representations', 'Oakmont Industrial Corp. provides its December 2025 management representation letter to the audit team at Webb & Associates CPA. Among the required representations, management should confirm regarding asset encumbrances that:', '["All assets have been disclosed to the auditor, including any liens, pledges, or other encumbrances on the entity''s assets", "No assets have depreciated below their original cost", "All fixed assets were acquired within the current fiscal year", "Asset valuations have been confirmed by independent appraisers"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under AU-C 580.11, management must represent that it has disclosed all liens, pledges, guarantees, and other encumbrances on the entity''s assets. This representation supports the auditor''s evaluation of the rights-and-obligations assertion. Stating that no assets have depreciated below cost (choice B) addresses valuation, not encumbrances, and is unrelated to the specific representation about liens and pledges.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q19: medium / L2 — Representations from those charged with governance
(1, 'Written Representations', 'While auditing Harborview Trust Co. for the year ended December 31, 2025, the engagement partner at Pryor & Associates determines that certain matters are within the direct knowledge of the board of directors rather than management. Under AU-C 580, the auditor should:', '["Include the board''s representations in the standard management representation letter", "Avoid requesting representations from the board since only management is required to provide them", "Request separate written representations from those charged with governance when the auditor determines that such representations are necessary", "Accept oral assurances from the board chair in lieu of written representations"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under AU-C 580.16, the auditor may request written representations from those charged with governance when matters fall within their direct responsibility or knowledge, such as approval of related-party transactions or oversight of financial reporting. Avoiding board representations entirely (choice B) is incorrect because AU-C 580 explicitly contemplates requesting representations from those charged with governance when relevant.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q20: easy / L1 — Representation about completeness of minutes
(1, 'Written Representations', 'Under AU-C 580, the management representation letter should confirm that management has provided the auditor with:', '["A list of all employees who have access to the accounting system", "A forecast of the next fiscal year''s operating budget", "A copy of all marketing materials distributed during the audit period", "Complete minutes of meetings of shareholders, directors, and committees of directors"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under AU-C 580.11, management must represent that it has provided complete minutes of meetings of shareholders, the board of directors, and committees of the board. These minutes may contain evidence of transactions, authorizations, and commitments affecting the financial statements. Providing employee access lists (choice A) relates to IT controls, not the specific written representation requirement regarding minutes.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q21: hard / L3 — Representation period extends through report date
(1, 'Written Representations', 'The audit team at Thornton & Reeves CPA completes fieldwork for Summit Ridge Corp.''s December 31, 2025 financial statements on February 20, 2026, but does not issue the auditor''s report until March 12, 2026. Management signs the representation letter on February 20. On March 5, the engagement partner discovers that Summit Ridge lost a major contract valued at $1.9 million. What is the most likely effect on the written representations?', '["The auditor should request an updated or dual-dated representation letter extending through the report date of March 12, 2026, addressing the lost contract", "The existing February 20 letter is sufficient because it was signed before the new event occurred", "The auditor should add a footnote to the existing letter referencing the lost contract", "The auditor should issue the report dated February 20 to avoid updating the representations"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under AU-C 580.14, the representation letter should be dated as of the date of the auditor''s report. When the report date is later than the letter date and a significant event occurs in between, the auditor should request updated or additional representations covering the period through the report date. Relying on the earlier letter (choice B) is incorrect because it does not cover events occurring between the letter date and the report date, leaving a gap in audit evidence.', 'hard', 3),

-- Q22: hard / L4 — Sole source of evidence for material contingent liability
(1, 'Written Representations', 'The audit of Prescott Biomedical Inc. for December 2025 reveals a $2.8 million contingent liability related to a patent infringement lawsuit. Management provides a written representation that the outcome is remote and no accrual or disclosure is necessary. The auditor at Whitfield & Greer CPA has not received a legal confirmation letter from Prescott''s outside counsel and has no other corroborating evidence. The auditor should conclude that:', '["The written representation alone provides sufficient evidence to support management''s assessment of the contingency", "The auditor cannot rely solely on management''s written representation for a material contingent liability and must obtain corroborating evidence, such as a legal confirmation, before forming a conclusion", "The auditor should accept the representation since management is in the best position to evaluate litigation outcomes", "The contingent liability should be ignored if management has signed the representation letter"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under AU-C 580.04, written representations are necessary but not sufficient appropriate audit evidence on their own. For a material contingent liability, the auditor must obtain corroborating evidence such as a legal confirmation letter (AU-C 501) before concluding on the assertion. Accepting the representation alone (choice A) violates the principle that representations supplement but do not replace other audit procedures, especially for material balances requiring independent corroboration.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q23: medium / L3 — Environmental liability representations
(1, 'Written Representations', 'Ironclad Manufacturing operates three plants near waterways and is subject to environmental regulations. During the December 2025 audit, the engagement team at Morrison & Blake CPA considers whether the management representation letter should address environmental matters. Under AU-C 580, which representation is most relevant to the environmental risk?', '["That the entity has never been inspected by environmental regulators", "That environmental compliance is solely the responsibility of the entity''s legal counsel", "That management has disclosed all known or probable environmental liabilities and remediation obligations affecting the financial statements", "That the entity has purchased environmental insurance coverage"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under AU-C 580.11 and AU-C 501, management should represent that it has disclosed all known or probable environmental liabilities, including remediation costs, that could affect the financial statements. This representation supports the completeness assertion for contingent liabilities. Stating the entity has never been inspected (choice A) does not address whether liabilities exist, since environmental liabilities can arise regardless of inspection history.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q24: easy / L1 — Plans and intentions affecting asset carrying amounts
(1, 'Written Representations', 'Under AU-C 580, why might the auditor request a representation about management''s plans or intentions?', '["To confirm that the entity intends to continue operating indefinitely", "To verify that all business strategies have been approved by shareholders", "To ensure the entity''s marketing plans are consistent with industry trends", "Because management''s plans may affect the carrying amounts or classification of assets and liabilities"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under AU-C 580.A13, the auditor may request representations about management''s plans and intentions because they can directly affect financial statement amounts and classifications — such as whether to hold or sell an investment, refinance debt, or dispose of a segment. Confirming indefinite operations (choice A) relates to going concern, which is a separate representation, not the general plans-and-intentions concept.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q25: easy / L1 — Oral representations should be confirmed in writing
(1, 'Written Representations', 'Under AU-C 580, when management makes oral representations during the audit, the auditor should:', '["Request that those representations be confirmed in writing in the management representation letter", "Treat oral representations as equivalent to written representations", "Disregard oral representations since they have no evidentiary value", "Record oral representations only if they contradict written evidence"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under AU-C 580.07, written representations are required for significant matters. Oral representations made during the audit should be confirmed in writing to ensure there is a clear, documented record. Treating oral and written representations as equivalent (choice B) is incorrect because written representations provide a more reliable and reviewable form of evidence than oral statements alone.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q26: medium / L2 — Title to assets representation
(1, 'Written Representations', 'During the audit of Silverstone Equipment Corp. for the year ended December 31, 2025, the engagement team at Baker & Holmes CPA reviews the management representation letter. Under AU-C 580, management should represent regarding assets that:', '["All assets have been appraised at fair value within the current year", "The entity has satisfactory title to all owned assets and has disclosed all liens or encumbrances", "Asset depreciation methods were selected by the audit committee", "All assets are insured for their full replacement cost"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under AU-C 580.11, management must represent that the entity holds satisfactory title to assets and has disclosed any liens, pledges, or other encumbrances. This representation supports the rights-and-obligations assertion for assets on the balance sheet. Requiring fair value appraisals (choice A) is incorrect because the standard does not mandate current-year appraisals; it requires disclosure of ownership and encumbrances.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q27: hard / L4 — Conflict between representation and legal confirmation
(1, 'Written Representations', 'Crestview Pharmaceutical Corp.''s management representation letter for December 2025 states that a pending product liability lawsuit is without merit and no provision or disclosure is needed. The auditor at Fenwick & Hale CPA has obtained a legal confirmation from outside counsel indicating a $3.2 million probable loss. The auditor should evaluate this conflict by:', '["Accepting management''s assessment because they are closer to the facts of the lawsuit", "Issuing an unmodified opinion with a note referencing the conflicting evidence", "Assessing the reliability of management''s representations overall, relying on the legal confirmation as higher-quality evidence, and considering the appropriate opinion modification if management refuses to adjust or disclose", "Withdrawing from the engagement immediately since management has provided a false representation"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under AU-C 580.A22 and AU-C 501, when external legal confirmation contradicts management''s representation about a contingency, the auditor must evaluate the conflict by considering the reliability hierarchy — external legal confirmation is more reliable than management''s assertion. The auditor should discuss the matter with management and evaluate the effect on the audit opinion. Accepting management''s assessment (choice A) is inappropriate because the legal confirmation constitutes higher-quality evidence that cannot be overridden by management''s representation alone.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q28: easy / L1 — Professional skepticism not reduced by representations
(1, 'Written Representations', 'Under AU-C 580, the auditor''s receipt of written representations from management:', '["Eliminates the need for the auditor to exercise professional skepticism regarding represented matters", "Reduces the auditor''s responsibility for detecting fraud in the financial statements", "Allows the auditor to reduce the scope of substantive testing by 50 percent", "Does not reduce the auditor''s obligation to exercise professional skepticism throughout the audit"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under AU-C 580.04 and AU-C 200.17, written representations supplement but do not replace the auditor''s professional skepticism. The auditor must continue to question and corroborate management''s assertions regardless of the representations received. Claiming that representations eliminate the need for skepticism (choice A) contradicts a fundamental principle of auditing — professional skepticism is maintained throughout the entire engagement.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q29: medium / L3 — Representations about debt covenant noncompliance
(1, 'Written Representations', 'Ridgeway Construction Corp. has a revolving credit facility with a debt-to-equity covenant of no more than 3:1. At December 31, 2025, the ratio is 3.4:1, triggering a technical default. Management tells the audit team at Crane & Associates CPA that the lender has verbally agreed to waive the violation. The auditor should analyze the representation letter to determine whether management has:', '["Certified that no debt covenants were violated during the year", "Confirmed that the verbal waiver constitutes a binding legal agreement", "Disclosed the noncompliance, the terms of the covenant, and the status of any waiver negotiations, including whether a written waiver has been obtained", "Stated that the covenant violation will be corrected in the following quarter"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under AU-C 580.11 and ASC 470-10, management must represent that it has disclosed all instances of noncompliance with debt covenants and any related waiver arrangements. A verbal waiver alone may not prevent reclassification of long-term debt to current. The auditor should evaluate whether a written waiver has been obtained. Certifying no violations occurred (choice A) would be inaccurate given the technical default and would constitute a misrepresentation in the letter.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q30: hard / L4 — Former management removed during year refuses representations
(1, 'Written Representations', 'Apex Technology Solutions terminated its CFO on September 15, 2025 for cause. The new CFO starts on October 1. During the December 2025 audit, the engagement partner at Prescott & Lane CPA requests representations from the former CFO covering January through September, but the former officer refuses. The auditor should evaluate this situation by:', '["Issuing a disclaimer of opinion for the full year because the former CFO refused to provide representations", "Obtaining representations from the current CFO covering the full fiscal year, performing additional procedures over the pre-October period, and evaluating whether sufficient appropriate audit evidence has been obtained", "Limiting the audit scope to October through December and disclosing the limitation", "Accepting the former CFO''s refusal and noting it as a subsequent event in the financial statements"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under AU-C 580.A11, the representation letter is obtained from current management, who is responsible for the financial statements covering the full period. The former officer''s refusal does not automatically require a disclaimer (choice A) because current management can provide representations over the full period based on their review and inquiry. However, the auditor should consider performing additional procedures to compensate for any increased risk related to the period before the management change.', 'hard', 4)
;

COMMIT;
